{"combines": true, "proposition": "Putting power factor (Obs A) and ZT (Obs B) together shows that the composition which maximizes PF (x=0.01, ~29 uW cm-1 K-2 at 300 K, ~22 at 623 K) is not the composition that maximizes ZT at 300 K (x=0.005, ~0.84, versus ~0.78 for x=0.01). So the two observations jointly establish that PF is a contributing factor to ZT but not the sole determinant of the ZT ranking across doping levels x=0, 0.005, 0.01, 0.02-0.04.", "limits": ["The panels give no thermal conductivity values, so the pair cannot show WHY the ZT peak shifts to x=0.005 while the PF peak stays at x=0.01 -- that requires the missing denominator term in ZT.", "Observation A spans two test temperatures (300 K and 623 K) while Observation B is reported only at 300 K (plus the n-Bi2Te3 reference curve), so the comparison is restricted to the 300 K point and says nothing about whether the PF/ZT composition mismatch holds at 623 K.", "PF is an absolute transport quantity while ZT is a normalized, dimensionless figure of merit built from PF together with other terms not shown here, so the two are different kinds of quantity and their numeric levels are not directly comparable, only their composition-wise orderings."], "not_identifiable": "Whether the higher ZT at x=0.005 despite its lower PF is due to a lower thermal conductivity at x=0.005 versus some other compositional effect on ZT cannot be separated using only the PF and ZT data given.", "quantity": {"a": {"kind": "rate/transport coefficient", "what": "power factor in uW cm-1 K-2, at 300 K and 623 K, across x=0, 0.005, 0.01, 0.02-0.04"}, "b": {"kind": "normalized, dimensionless figure of merit", "what": "ZT at 300 K across x=0.005, 0.01, 0.02-0.04, plus an n-Bi2Te3 reference curve (~0.88)"}}, "labels": {"dependency": "real", "inference_validity": "follows, weakly", "causal_strength": "conditional mechanism"}}